special_abilities:
  - name: "Life Sense"
    description: "Senses living things through the bond with nature, twenty times more keenly than an ordinary Lemurian. One melee action of concentration pinpoints a chosen kind of animal or plant in range: 35% +5% per level of experience (+5% underwater). A hiding animal may be felt as near without being located. The Gene-Mage also knows when a creature or plant is dying, why, and roughly how long it has. Undead and evil supernatural beings register loudly, with their proximity and general location. Range: 500 foot (152 m) radius plus 50 feet (15.2 m) per level. Adds +10% to the Tracking, Hunting and Identify Plants and Fruit skills."
  - name: "Sense Death and the Unnatural"
    description: "Automatic and costs no P.P.E. or I.S.P. Supernatural evil, the undead, death and large-scale damage to nature (dead forest, deforestation, strip mining, poisoned water, radiation) are felt as a sickening nausea. The reading is general: presence and a rough direction, growing stronger with nearness; whether the destruction is minor, serious, major or horrific; whether supernatural evil is lesser, greater or deific, few or many, near or far, approaching, waiting or leaving. Range: 500 feet (152 m) plus 100 feet (30.5 m) per level of experience. The range is four times that for evil of extraordinary power: the Lord of the Deep and its Reachers, any other hostile Alien Intelligence, and beings on a god''s scale such as the Splugorth."
  - name: "Psionic Empathy with Animals"
    description: "The same as every Lemurian (printed 48): animals treat the character as a fellow animal. Domesticated animals like him at once, giving +10% to ride any horse or other non-predatory land or sea animal and to skills for working with domestic animals. Wild animals other than monsters, felines, sharks and mutant or alien predators do not flee, sound an alarm or turn hostile unless he threatens them. Predators and monsters treat a Lemurian as a rival and tend to attack him first."
  - name: "Communicate with Plants and Animals"
    description: "Wordless, empathy-like contact with nearby land and sea plants and animals - sea mammals, fish, mollusks, crustaceans, coral, plankton, algae and the like. It yields flashes of past or current events limited to what the creature can sense: a tree or reef feels weight, heat, sound and vibration but sees no faces; an animal gives visual impressions from its own point of view. Communication is automatic. Calming an enraged or frightened animal: 35% +5% per level of experience (+10% with sea creatures); half if the animal is alien to Earth or has already been attacked or injured, one quarter if it is in a blood frenzy or injured and in pain. No effect on supernatural monsters, creatures of magic or intelligent beings. Range: 60 feet (18.3 m) plus 10 feet (3 m) per level, increased by 50% underwater for a Lemurian Gene-Mage. With the Lemurian''s own empathic connection with animals, a Lemurian Gene-Mage has +15% on any skill that relates to or interacts with animals, such as Horsemanship and Animal Husbandry. The opening paragraph on printed 63 gives different figures for the same ability: a calming chance of 40% +5% per level and a range of 120 feet (36.6 m)."
  - name: "Healing Touch"
    description: "A touch and the will to heal restores 1D6 S.D.C./Hit Points or 1D4 M.D.C. per level of experience to one plant, animal or person, the Gene-Mage included. Costs no P.P.E. or I.S.P. Three times per 24 hours at level one, four at level 5, five at level 10 and six at level 15."
  - name: "Gifts of Nature"
    description: "For 8 P.P.E., part of a living plant becomes a fruit-like Gift that feeds one person for a day; it works above and below the waves, seaweed and algae included. Only trees, bushes or larger plants, or a dense patch of seaweed or algae at least 10 feet (3 m) across, can be used, and each gives a limited number: a bush or a 10 foot seaweed patch 1, a man-sized plant 1D4, a medium tree 1D4+2, the largest trees 1D6x10. The Gene-Mage senses the limit. Exceeding it does 2D6 S.D.C. to the plant and a 1D4x10 S.D.C. psychic backlash to the mage (M.D. to a Mega-Damage being or a Biomancer in Bio-Armor). Cannot be used on Millennium Trees or any sentient plant; trying it on a Millennium Tree injures the Tree and sets off a Mega-Damage explosion."
  - name: "Gift of Peace"
    description: "An aura keeps animals of every size from attacking the Gene-Mage except in self-defense; undersea this covers fish, jellyfish and sharks, even sharks in a blood frenzy, which carry on but leave him alone even when the blood in the water is his own. Predators and some alien monsters of animal intelligence, such as mutant sharks and dinosaur-like creatures, attack only if cornered, injured, attacked first, extremely hungry and desperate, or if he cannot defend himself. The Gift does nothing against people, other intelligent creatures, demons, creatures of magic or supernatural monsters."
  - name: "Create Bio-Constructs"
    description: "Weapons, armor and equipment are made by magically altering living animals and plants without hurting them, forming a symbiotic union the Biomancer can wear or use. Advanced constructs call for real knowledge of biology and genetics. Bio-Armor and engineered war steeds are the work of high-ranking Gene-Mages with assistants and special facilities; those rituals are guarded secrets and are not available to standard player characters. The starting Bio-Constructs are listed under equipment."
  - name: "Plant P.P.E."
    description: "Only the Gene-Mage can draw the small P.P.E. held in plants, one acre at a time, or 1D4+1 large or ancient trees at a time; add one more acre at levels 4, 8 and 12. P.P.E. taken is not available again for 24 hours. Per acre per day: grasses, flowers, ferns, small shrubs, algae, seaweed and other small plants 1D4 (+2 if bearing fruit); medium to large shrubs, small trees and columns of seaweed 1D6 (+1D6 if fruiting); fully grown trees 3D6 (+1D6 if fruiting); coral up to 3D6. Large (over 100 feet), ancient (over 200 years) or giant trees give up to 2D4+1 each, plus 1D4 per 1,000 years of age. The Great Barrier Reef is a mysterious being, not true coral, and is the exception. P.P.E. is also drawn from ley lines, nexus points and other people as a Ley Line Walker does."
  - name: "Perception and Spell Strength"
    description: "+4 on Perception Rolls involving Biomancy, symbiotic organisms and genetic manipulation, and half that for other magicks and nature. +1 to Spell Strength (the number others must roll to save against the Gene-Mage''s spells) at levels 3, 6, 9 and 12."
  - name: "Spell Knowledge"
    description: "Magic grows from the link with nature, much as a Mystic''s does. Level 1: six Biomancy spells from spell levels 1-4, plus three Ocean Magic spells. Level 2: four spells from levels 1-5, Biomancy or Ocean Magic. Level 3: four Biomancy spells from levels 1-6, and two Ocean Magic or common Invocation spells. Level 4 and every level after: two Biomancy spells from any spell level up to four above the character''s own. The invocations are the list printed on 106-107; a Gene-Mage may study Ocean Magic in place of those invocations, but not both. Ocean and water Biomancy spells and the Bio-Armor spells belong to Lemurian Biomancers alone (printed 87)."
restrictions:
  - "Race: Gene-Mage Biomancy is known only to Lemurians and the few they trust. 94% of Gene-Mages are Lemurian, 5% are Merans and 1% are rare others, a few humans among them. It is taught only to those the Lemurians believe will do great good with it and who share their philosophy of life."
  - "Alignment: any, but Principled (30%), Scrupulous (35%) and Unprincipled (12%) are the most common."
  - "A P.E. of 13 or higher is suggested but not required."
  - "Cybernetics: none. Lemurians have no interest in bionics, not even Bio-Systems."
  - "Never uses Rune Weapons, and abhors Splugorth Bio-Wizardry. Most Gene-Mages are vegetarian and avoid machines, electronics and cities."
  - "Starting gear also includes access to every type of Bio-Skin, 1D4 Lemurian War Steeds (only one usable at a time, and rarely lent out) and one animal companion, usually an Aquatic Lemur."
  - "Also carried: a cloak, box of wooden matches, mess kit, language translator, distancing binoculars, 2D6+6 plastic specimen bags, a pocket notebook, 20 feet (6.1 m) of rope or cord and common personal items. Fanatically anti-tech Gene-Mages replace things like binoculars and gas masks with symbiotes or magical equivalents."
  - "Money: a monthly salary of 3,200 credits or trade goods on top of the starting savings. Most have a family estate in a floating city or Lemurian Garden; those working with the military may use barracks."
extraction_notes: |
  - Rifts World Book 32: Lemuria, printed 60-66 (cache p061-p067). The entry
    starts on printed 60 and its Cybernetics and Money lines finish on printed
    66, one page past the 60-65 the task named. Ladder printed 86 (cache p087),
    read off a render: the column headed "Gene-Mage & Milu".
  - men_of_arms false and occ_group magic: the section heading is "Lemurian
    Practitioner of Magic O.C.C." (printed 60). No S.D.C. or hit point formula
    is printed.
  - THREE SCHOOLS (printed 61-62): Acceptors, Patient Ones, Defenders. The book
    says the O.C.C. abilities and skills are fundamentally the same for each
    and only the world view differs, so there are no variants and no pick
    group; the schools are a prose section in the body.
  - SPELLS, as printed 65: level 1 six Biomancy (levels 1-4) plus three Ocean
    Magic; level 2 four from levels 1-5, Biomancy or Ocean; level 3 four
    Biomancy (levels 1-6) and two Ocean Magic or common Invocation; level 4
    and after, two Biomancy per level from any level up to four above the
    character''s own. Stored as named lists, because a level-gated pick would
    also reach every general invocation and the book limits each pick to named
    families: BIO_START (the 46 Biomancy rows of levels 1-4; a starting group
    bounded by a list takes no level cap, so the cap is in the list), BIO (86),
    OCEAN (49), BIO_OCEAN, OCEAN_INVOC (Ocean plus the 85 invocations).
    Schedule entries carry both the list and the spell-level cap.
  - BIO is every catalog row of tradition biomancy except Biomancy: Woodland
    Entity, which is South America''s and is not in Lemuria''s alphabetical
    listing (printed 86-87). All 86 others were matched to that listing; the
    book''s Animal Phantom is the held Biomancy: Animal Ghost and its
    Strengthen Plant the held Biomancy: Strengthen Plants.
  - OCEAN is every catalog row of tradition ocean. The book says only "Ocean
    Magic spells" and gives no level limit for the three starting ones or the
    two at level 3; none was invented.
  - Invocations (printed 106-107), moved to the catalog''s spelling: Breathe
    without Air -> Breathe Without Air; Swim as a Fish -> Swim as a Fish
    (lesser); Reduce Self -> Reduce Self (6 inches); Dispel Magic Barrier ->
    Dispel Magic Barriers; Invisibility: Superior -> Invisibility (Superior);
    Summon and Control Canine -> Summon and Control Canines; Summon and
    Control Sea Serpents -> Summon & Control Sea Serpents; Life ward ->
    Lifeward. All 85 matched a general-invocation row at the level printed.
  - "Ocean Magic as an alternative ... instead of selecting some of the spell
    invocations listed below, but not both" (printed 106) is carried in the
    note on the level 3 pick and in Spell Knowledge; nothing enforces it.
  - Printed 87: the ocean/water Biomancy spells and the Bio-Armor spells are
    for Lemurian Biomancers only (not South America''s Biomancers, not Ocean
    Mages). This class is the Lemurian one, so its lists hold them all; the
    rule is stated in Spell Knowledge.
  - Bonuses stored: +2 Horror Factor, +20% coma/death, +1 disease, +3 vs
    Bio-Manipulation magic (saves.other), +1 save vs magic at levels 4, 7, 10,
    12 and 15 on both spell_magic and ritual_magic. Prose only: the +4
    Perception (conditional) and +1 Spell Strength at 3, 6, 9, 12.
  - Skills Known by All Lemurians are printed inside this stat block and are
    stored as O.C.C. skills at the printed figures. Language: Lemurian and
    Oceanic are printed 96% +0.5% per level; a fractional rate is not stored,
    so per_level is 0 and each note carries the printed rate. Literacy:
    Lemurian is the catalog row of that name, at the printed 86/+1.
  - Mathematics: Basic is printed twice: 82% +2% per level under the Lemurian
    skills, and (+20%) under O.C.C. Skills. Stored once at 98 (82 + 20 = 102,
    held to the 98% ceiling) with per_level 2. A judgement.
  - Bases added: Biology 30+20, Botany 25+30, Chemistry 30+10, Lore: Cattle &
    Animals 30+25 (book: Lore: Animals/Cattle), Lore: Demons & Monsters 25+15,
    Lore: Sea Creatures 25+20, Marine Biology 35+30, Mathematics: Advanced
    45+15, Sea Holistic Medicine 20+25, Undersea & Sea Survival 25+10. Lore:
    Vampires is printed outright as 60% +3%.
  - Hand to Hand: Expert for two related skills, Martial Arts three, Kapu
    Kuialua four; Kapu Kuialua is the catalog''s Hand to Hand: Demon Combat.
  - Related skills: four at level one, one more at 4, 8, 10 and 14. Secondary:
    three, one more at 3, 5, 9 and 13.
  - "Aquatic: Any (+5%)": the catalog has no Aquatic category. Stored as one
    related-skill entry named Aquatic at +5 whose `only` list is the book''s
    Aquatic Skills list (printed 54) by catalog name. Left out of it: the six
    this class already grants as O.C.C. skills (Language: Lemurian, Language:
    Oceanic, Lore: Sea Creatures, Marine Biology, Sea Holistic Medicine,
    Undersea & Sea Survival) and Horsemanship: Serpent Hunter, which is the
    Serpent Hunter''s own. Several of the ten that remain are also inside a
    category this class opens at a higher printed bonus (Technical, Science
    and Wilderness at +10%).
  - Communication +10% names Creative Writing and Language, which the catalog
    files under Technical (already +10% here). Medical "except Cybernetic
    Medicine" excludes both Cybernetic Medicine and M.D. in Cybernetics.
    Rogue "Gambling" is Gambling (Standard) only. "Electrical: Basic only" is
    Basic Electronics; "Mechanical: Basic only" is Basic Mechanics. W.P.
    "except Heavy Weapons" excludes W.P. Heavy Military Weapons and W.P. Heavy
    M.D. Weapons. Pilot "except military ones" excludes the rows prefixed
    Military; Jet Pack (Squid Pack) is Jet Packs.
  - Equipment: Bio-Armor "ANY; select two" is a pick of two from the ten
    Bio-Armor rows. The melee Bio-Weapon pick is the six Bio-Weapon families
    plus the Crab Claw Gauntlet and Tentacle Sword, less the two Serpent
    Bio-Weapons (the Serpent Hunter''s alone); the long-range pick is the seven
    ranged rows left once the two Stonecasters (Men-At-Arms only) are taken
    out. The Sonic Blast Staff and Serpent Entangler Net are in neither. The
    "1D4+1 other Symbiotic Bio-Constructs of choice" is a pick of two, the
    minimum of the roll, from the seven symbiote rows not granted outright. Small flashlight -> pocket-flashlight; laser distancer ->
    pocket-laser-distancer; sketchbook -> sketch-pad; medium sack -> sack;
    casual clothes -> clothing; satchel -> purse-satchel; rope row is per 40
    ft where the book gives 20 ft. Items with no single matching row are in
    restrictions as prose.
  - Money: 2D6x1,000 credits in savings stored; the 3,200 credit monthly
    salary is prose.
  - Healing Touch uses per day change by level (3/4/5/6 at 1/5/10/15), so it
    is not a trackable resource with a fixed maximum.
  - Ability 4 prints two sets of figures (printed 63: 40% +5% and 120 feet;
    printed 64: 35% +5% and 60 feet +10 per level). Both are in the ability.
---

## Lore

The Biomancer Gene-Mage is Lemuria''s practitioner of magic: a mage whose power comes from a bond with living things rather than from study of spell books. Lemurian Biomancy is a more developed form of the art known to the Jungle Elves of South America, shaped for life both on land and beneath the sea. Its practitioners grow what their people need - weapons, tools, armor, riding animals - out of living matter, without machines and without killing.

That bond has a price. A Gene-Mage gives up city comforts and technology, usually eats no meat, and treats sea creatures, plants and animals as kin. Many will not enter a city unless they must. The magic is the opposite of Necromancy, and its practitioners are the sworn enemies of the undead, of Splugorth Bio-Wizardry and of anyone who makes or carries Rune Weapons.

In play the Gene-Mage is a healer, scout of the natural world and support caster with a deep spell list, a handful of Ocean Magic, living armor and a stable of war steeds.

## The Three Schools

All three schools share the same abilities and skills; they differ in outlook. Acceptors tolerate most of what civilization does to nature as part of evolution and object only to the worst of it; they will live near cities and use some technology, though not cybernetics, Techno-Wizardry, Bio-Wizardry or Rune Weapons. Patient Ones accept none of it but take no action beyond repairing damage and defending their own lands and waters; they use technology only as a last resort, and most Lemurians and Gene-Mages are of this school. Defenders, about 7% of Gene-Mages, reject technology outright, actively stop those who harm nature, and punish those who kill or destroy for pleasure or profit.

## GM Notes

The rituals that create Bio-Armor and war steeds belong to high-ranking NPC Gene-Mages with assistants and facilities, not to player characters. The class is taught outside Lemuria only rarely, to Merans and a very few others the Lemurians trust.
